  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://cs.unb.ca/ontologies/riskonto#T1164">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://cs.unb.ca/ontologies/riskonto#AttackTechnique"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://cs.unb.ca/ontologies/riskonto#EnterpriseTechnique"/>
    <rdfs:label>Re-opened Applications</rdfs:label>
    <riskonto:description>Starting in Mac OS X 10.7 (Lion), users can specify certain applications to be re-opened when a user reboots their machine. While this is usually done via a Graphical User Interface (GUI) on an app-by-app basis, there are property list files (plist) that contain this information as well located at &lt;code&gt;~/Library/Preferences/com.apple.loginwindow.plist&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;~/Library/Preferences/ByHost/com.apple.loginwindow.* .plist&lt;/code&gt;. 

An adversary can modify one of these files directly to include a link to their malicious executable to provide a persistence mechanism each time the user reboots their machine (Citation: Methods of Mac Malware Persistence).</riskonto:description>
    <rdfs:comment>Starting in Mac OS X 10.7 (Lion), users can specify certain applications to be re-opened when a user reboots their machine. While this is usually done via a Graphical User Interface (GUI) on an app-by-app basis, there are property list files (plist) that contain this information as well located at &lt;code&gt;~/Library/Preferences/com.apple.loginwindow.plist&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;~/Library/Preferences/ByHost/com.apple.loginwindow.* .plist&lt;/code&gt;. 

An adversary can modify one of these files directly to include a link to their malicious executable to provide a persistence mechanism each time the user reboots their machine (Citation: Methods of Mac Malware Persistence).</rdfs:comment>
    <riskonto:techniqueID>T1164</riskonto:techniqueID>
    <riskonto:externalID>T1164</riskonto:externalID>
